\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Scaling laws, data repetition, low-rank adaptation (LoRA), and key-value (KV) caching are well studied in large language models, but it is unclear whether their benefits hold for models with only a few million parameters. We train decoder-only Transformers from scratch on TinyStories (904M tokens) and run five controlled, seeded experiments on a single laptop GPU. With a fixed budget of 100M training tokens, shrinking the unique data from 100M to 8M tokens (12.5 epochs) leaves test perplexity almost unchanged (5.20 compared with 5.19), and even 50 epochs over 2M tokens costs only about 2\%. Across seven model sizes from 75K to 8.8M parameters, test loss follows the power law $L = 0.71 + 103.7\,N^{-0.37}$. When adapting a 1.78M-parameter model to an instruction task, LoRA at rank 16 recovers 94\% of the perplexity gain of full fine-tuning with 14.7\% of the trainable parameters and forgets less. However, no method learns to follow explicit word constraints (0.6\% to 1.9\% success, compared with 92.8\% for reference stories). At this width LoRA also trains more slowly than full fine-tuning, and it becomes faster ($1.84\times$) only at embedding dimension 1024. KV caching gives no reliable speedup for short generations but reaches $1.85\times$ at batch size 16 with 490 tokens. These results show that parameter, data, training-time, and inference-time efficiency are separate properties that do not automatically improve together at this scale.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Tiny language models, scaling laws, data-constrained scaling, LoRA, parameter-efficient fine-tuning, KV cache, inference efficiency.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Small language models trained on simplified corpora offer an inexpensive way to study how language models behave. TinyStories \cite{eldan2023tinystories} showed that models far smaller than production systems can still write coherent text when trained on a restricted vocabulary with simple narrative structure. We use the informal term TinyLMs for decoder-only Transformers with roughly a hundred thousand to a few million parameters, which are small enough that controlled experiments can be repeated with several seeds on one laptop GPU.

Research on efficiency usually targets the opposite end of the scale. It covers scaling laws \cite{kaplan2020scaling,hoffmann2022chinchilla}, repeated versus fresh data \cite{muennighoff2023dataconstrained}, parameter-efficient fine-tuning such as LoRA \cite{hu2022lora,houlsby2019peft}, and KV caching \cite{shazeer2019multiquery,pope2022efficiently}. These techniques were developed for models with hundreds of millions to hundreds of billions of parameters, and they need not behave the same way in tiny models. When each layer does little computation, fixed overheads such as kernel launches, memory allocation, and framework bookkeeping take up a larger share of the total cost.

This paper does not propose a new method. Instead, it asks a focused empirical question: when scaling, data repetition, LoRA, and KV caching are applied to TinyLMs under controlled conditions, do they behave as their reputation at large scale would suggest? We break this into five research questions.

\textbf{RQ1 (data).} For a fixed number of training tokens, how much does the amount of \emph{unique} data matter? In other words, how costly is it to repeat a smaller pool?

\textbf{RQ2 (model).} Does test loss improve steadily as models grow from 75K to 8.8M parameters, and can a power law describe that improvement?

\textbf{RQ3 (LoRA, quality).} When LoRA and full fine-tuning produce models with the same final architecture and parameter count, how much of the full fine-tuning gain does LoRA recover, and how does it affect forgetting and instruction following?

\textbf{RQ4 (LoRA, speed).} Does LoRA train faster than full fine-tuning at TinyLM scale? If it does not, at what model size does it start to?

\textbf{RQ5 (KV cache).} Does KV caching give a meaningful end-to-end speedup for TinyLM generation, and how does that depend on batch size and generation length?

All five experiments share one tokenizer, data split, seeding procedure, and evaluation protocol. Our main finding is that parameter efficiency, data efficiency, training speed, and inference speed are distinct properties at this scale. In particular, LoRA does not train faster until the model is wide enough, and KV caching helps only for long, batched generations.

\section{Background and Related Work}

Our models follow the standard decoder-only GPT design \cite{vaswani2017attention,radford2019gpt2}. Token embeddings and learned positional embeddings pass through $L$ pre-normalized blocks, each containing causal multi-head self-attention and a feed-forward network with residual connections. Attention is computed as
\begin{equation}
\text{Attn}(Q,K,V) = \text{softmax}\!\left(\frac{QK^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}} + M\right)V,
\end{equation}
where the mask $M$ blocks attention to future positions. The output layer shares its weights with the input embeddings. The architecture is standard and is not a contribution of this paper.

\textbf{Scaling and data.} Kaplan et al.\ \cite{kaplan2020scaling} showed that loss follows power laws in model size, data, and compute, and Hoffmann et al.\ \cite{hoffmann2022chinchilla} argued that model size and data should grow together. Muennighoff et al.\ \cite{muennighoff2023dataconstrained} found that, for models hundreds of times larger than ours, up to about four epochs of repeated data are nearly as useful as new data. All of this work operates far above our scale. The BabyLM challenge \cite{warstadt2023babylm} also studies small data and models, but it targets pretraining quality rather than efficiency trade-offs.

\textbf{LoRA} \cite{hu2022lora} freezes a pretrained matrix $W_0 \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times k}$ and learns a low-rank update, $W = W_0 + \frac{\alpha}{r}BA$, with $B \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times r}$, $A \in \mathbb{R}^{r \times k}$, and $r \ll \min(d,k)$. Two properties matter here. First, the frozen matrix still takes part in every forward pass and the low-rank path adds extra computation, so any wall-clock saving depends on the setting. Second, the update can be merged into $W_0$ after training, so a LoRA model has exactly the same architecture as a fully fine-tuned one, which allows a direct comparison. At large scale, LoRA and adapters \cite{houlsby2019peft} match full fine-tuning in quality with far fewer trainable parameters \cite{hu2022lora}. We test quality and speed separately and also measure forgetting and constraint following, which such studies rarely report.

\textbf{KV caching} stores the keys and values of earlier positions so that each generation step only computes them for the newest token \cite{shazeer2019multiquery,pope2022efficiently}. It removes repeated computation but adds memory use and bookkeeping. Serving research usually assumes large models and long contexts, whereas we study short generations from small models.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\textbf{Data.} We use TinyStories \cite{eldan2023tinystories} and TinyStoriesInstruct. The TinyStories training split has 2,109,719 stories (904.5M tokens). From the official validation split, we use 10,000 stories (4.27M tokens) for development and the remaining 21,990 (9.17M tokens) for testing. TinyStoriesInstruct provides 294,993 training, 5,000 development, and 4,999 test examples (153.5M, 2.60M, and 2.48M tokens). Each Instruct example begins with a header of \texttt{Features:}, \texttt{Words:}, and \texttt{Summary:} fields ending in \texttt{Story:}, and the training loss is computed only on the story. We apply NFKC normalization, remove non-printable characters, and collapse whitespace, with no truncation, filtering, or deduplication. About 5.6\% of validation stories contain a garbled quotation sequence (\texttt{\^{a}\texteuro}) from a decoding error in the original release, which we left in place.

\textbf{Tokenizer.} We trained a byte-pair-encoding tokenizer \cite{sennrich2016bpe} on 200,000 training stories, giving 1,022 learned tokens plus \texttt{<unk>} and \texttt{<|endoftext|>}. Tokens never cross word boundaries. Because every experiment uses this tokenizer, it cannot explain differences between conditions.

\textbf{Models and training.} For model scaling we vary depth $L$ and set the number of heads to $L$ and the width to $16L$, giving 74,624 to 8,814,848 parameters. Experiments 1 and 3 use the 8-layer model (128-dimensional embeddings, 1,783,040 parameters). We train with AdamW \cite{loshchilov2019decoupled} ($\beta = (0.9, 0.95)$, weight decay 0.01 on matrices only), gradient clipping at 1.0, dropout 0.1, context length 512, and batch size 32. The learning rate warms up linearly over 5\% to 10\% of steps and then decays with a cosine schedule to 10\% of its peak. Every run is fully seeded, and a seeded epoch-based sampler ensures that runs with the same seed see identical batches. For each model size in Experiment 2 and each method in Experiment 3, we select the learning rate from a fixed grid using short development-set probes that share the same initialization and batches.

\textbf{Evaluation and timing.} We compute test loss and perplexity exactly over every non-overlapping window of a fixed initial portion of each test split: the first 2M TinyStories test tokens in Experiments 1 and 2, and the first 1M tokens of each test set in Experiment 3. Multi-seed results are reported as means with 95\% confidence intervals (3 seeds in Experiments 1 and 2, 5 in Experiment 3). All experiments ran on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050 6GB Laptop GPU with PyTorch 2.5.1 (CUDA) and Python 3.11.13. Timing excludes warmup, calls \texttt{torch.cuda.synchronize()} before each clock reading, and interleaves conditions in shuffled order so that thermal or scheduling drift cannot favor any condition. We report medians over repeats. The fine-tuned checkpoints from Experiment 3 were re-evaluated in a separate process and reproduced their stored perplexities to three decimal places. Every run saves its configuration, seed, git commit, learning curve, and final metrics as JSON, and all tables and figures are generated from these files by script rather than typed by hand.

\section{Experiment 1: Data Scaling at a Fixed Budget}
\label{sec:exp1}

We train the 1.78M-parameter model on 100M tokens (6,103 steps) drawn from unique pools of 2M, 8M, 32M, or 100M tokens. Smaller pools are repeated and reshuffled each epoch, so the 2M pool is seen 50 times. Each pool is taken from the start of the training file, and the learning rate is fixed at $3\times10^{-4}$ so that data is the only variable we change.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Test perplexity with a fixed 100M-token budget and varying amounts of unique data (mean $\pm$ 95\% CI, 3 seeds).}
\label{tab:data}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
Unique tokens & Epochs & Test PPL & $\Delta$ vs.\ 100M \\
\midrule
2M   & 50.0  & $5.29 \pm 0.05$ & $+1.9\%$ \\
8M   & 12.5  & $5.20 \pm 0.03$ & $+0.1\%$ \\
32M  & 3.1   & $5.18 \pm 0.08$ & $-0.2\%$ \\
100M & 1.0   & $5.19 \pm 0.03$ & reference \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Almost all of the benefit of fresh data is present by 8M unique tokens (Table~\ref{tab:data}). Growing the pool from 2M to 8M tokens improves perplexity by 1.7\%, while growing it from 8M to 100M changes perplexity by less than the variation between seeds. Even fifty passes over 2M tokens cost only about 2\%. This agrees in direction with findings for much larger models \cite{muennighoff2023dataconstrained}, where a few epochs of repetition cost very little. Our models, however, tolerate far more repetition: at this size and training length, they come nowhere near memorizing even heavily repeated data. For RQ1, unique data helps only up to a point, after which repetition is a cheap and effective substitute.

\section{Experiment 2: Model Scaling}
\label{sec:exp2}

We train seven model sizes from scratch, each for one pass over 200M tokens (12,207 steps). For each size, the learning rate is chosen from $\{3\times10^{-4}, 10^{-3}, 3\times10^{-3}\}$, and we train three seeds.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Test loss after one pass over 200M tokens (mean $\pm$ 95\% CI, 3 seeds). The learning rate for each size was selected on development probes.}
\label{tab:scaling}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}
\toprule
$L$ & Width & Params & Best LR & Test loss & PPL \\
\midrule
2  & 32  & 74,624    & $3{\times}10^{-3}$ & $2.322 \pm 0.025$ & 10.20 \\
4  & 64  & 298,368   & $3{\times}10^{-3}$ & $1.702 \pm 0.086$ & 5.48 \\
6  & 96  & 818,688   & $3{\times}10^{-3}$ & $1.348 \pm 0.002$ & 3.85 \\
8  & 128 & 1,783,040 & $1{\times}10^{-3}$ & $1.225 \pm 0.003$ & 3.40 \\
10 & 160 & 3,338,880 & $3{\times}10^{-3}$ & $1.089 \pm 0.004$ & 2.97 \\
12 & 192 & 5,633,664 & $1{\times}10^{-3}$ & $1.045 \pm 0.004$ & 2.84 \\
14 & 224 & 8,814,848 & $1{\times}10^{-3}$ & $0.995 \pm 0.005$ & 2.70 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{results/figs/fig_e2_scaling_law.png}
\caption{Test loss against parameter count (log scale) with the fitted power law $L(N) = 0.71 + 103.7\,N^{-0.37}$. Error bars show 95\% CIs over 3 seeds.}
\label{fig:scaling}
\end{figure}

Test loss decreases with every increase in size across the full $118\times$ range, with no plateau (Table~\ref{tab:scaling}, Fig.~\ref{fig:scaling}). The standard form $L(N) = E + A\,N^{-\alpha}$ \cite{kaplan2020scaling} fits well, with $E = 0.71$, $A = 103.7$, and $\alpha = 0.37$. With only seven points, the bootstrap 95\% intervals are wide (0.22 to 0.57 for $\alpha$, 0.30 to 0.91 for $E$), so we treat the curve as a description of the trend rather than a precise exponent. For RQ2, loss improves smoothly and predictably with capacity even at the smallest sizes, with no sign of a floor.

\section{Experiment 3: LoRA vs.\ Full Fine-Tuning}
\label{sec:exp3}

We adapt the seed-0, 8-layer model from Experiment 2 (test perplexity 3.40 on TinyStories and 3.65 on Instruct) to TinyStoriesInstruct, a task with a measurable behavior rather than only a shift in text style. Every method trains for 1,220 steps (20M tokens) from an identical copy of the base model, with 5 seeds and a learning rate chosen per method. LoRA is applied to all four linear layers per block with $\alpha = 2r$ and merged into the base weights after training. We measure Instruct test perplexity, TinyStories test perplexity as a measure of forgetting, and a Words-constraint rate: the fraction of 500 held-out prompts whose generated story contains all three required words. Reference stories reach 92.8\% on this measure.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{LoRA vs.\ full fine-tuning on TinyStoriesInstruct (means over 5 seeds; 95\% CIs are at most 0.002 for perplexity and 0.7 points for Words). Forget: TinyStories perplexity after adaptation.}
\label{tab:lora}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}
\toprule
Method & Train.\ par.\ (\%) & PPL & Forget & Words & Time \\
\midrule
Baseline   & 0 (0\%)        & 3.650 & 3.398 & 0.0\%  & 0s \\
LoRA $r{=}1$  & 16K (0.9\%)   & 3.287 & 3.444 & 0.6\%  & 306s \\
LoRA $r{=}2$  & 33K (1.8\%)   & 3.268 & 3.447 & 0.6\%  & 313s \\
LoRA $r{=}4$  & 66K (3.7\%)   & 3.256 & 3.449 & 0.6\%  & 313s \\
LoRA $r{=}8$  & 131K (7.3\%)  & 3.250 & 3.453 & 1.3\%  & 312s \\
LoRA $r{=}16$ & 262K (14.7\%) & 3.230 & 3.444 & 1.9\%  & 308s \\
LoRA $r{=}32$ & 524K (29.4\%) & 3.221 & 3.447 & 1.7\%  & 317s \\
LoRA $r{=}64$ & 1.05M (58.8\%)& 3.211 & 3.442 & 1.8\%  & 323s \\
Full FT    & 1.78M (100\%)  & \textbf{3.201} & 3.474 & 1.4\%  & \textbf{254s} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:lora} supports four findings. First, LoRA is very effective with few parameters. Rank 1 (0.9\% of parameters) recovers 81\% of the perplexity gain of full fine-tuning, and rank 16 (14.7\%) recovers 94\%. Second, full fine-tuning reaches the lowest perplexity but forgets the most, with a TinyStories perplexity of 3.474 compared with 3.44 to 3.45 for every LoRA rank. Third, and most notably, the behavioral metric barely changes. Every method, including full fine-tuning, includes all three required words in only 0.6\% to 1.9\% of stories, far below the 92.8\% ceiling. Twenty million tokens of instruction tuning taught the models the style of the task but not how to follow explicit constraints. Differences between methods at these low rates are within noise, since each was tested on 500 prompts with one sample each. Fourth, and this motivates Experiment 4, LoRA takes longer to train than full fine-tuning at every rank (306 to 323 seconds compared with 254 seconds), even though it trains up to $109\times$ fewer parameters.

The learning-rate sweeps also show a consistent pattern. The best learning rate for LoRA decreases as the rank increases, from $10^{-2}$ at ranks 1 through 8 to $10^{-3}$ at rank 64, moving toward the $3{\times}10^{-4}$ preferred by full fine-tuning. As the rank grows, LoRA behaves more like ordinary training.

For RQ3, LoRA offers large savings in trainable parameters and slightly reduces forgetting, full fine-tuning still achieves the best perplexity, and neither produces meaningful constraint following at this scale. Training fewer parameters also did not make training faster, which we investigate next.

\section{Experiment 4: When Does LoRA Train Faster?}
\label{sec:exp4}

A likely explanation for the timing result is that at width 128 the frozen matrices are cheap, so LoRA saves little gradient computation while its low-rank path adds a fixed cost to every layer. If so, the ranking should reverse as width grows. We time training steps for 8-layer models of width 64 to 1024 (0.5M to 102M parameters, head dimension 64), comparing full fine-tuning with LoRA at ranks 8 and 64. Since we measure computation rather than learning, we use random weights and inputs. Each condition runs 50 warmup and 200 timed steps at batch size 16 and is repeated 5 times in shuffled order.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Median training-step time by width (8 layers, batch size 16, context 512). Speedup is full fine-tuning time divided by LoRA time.}
\label{tab:width}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrrr}
\toprule
Width & Params & Full & LoRA $r{=}8$ & LoRA $r{=}64$ & $r{=}8$ speedup \\
\midrule
64   & 0.50M  & 0.108s & 0.121s & 0.127s & $0.89\times$ \\
128  & 1.78M  & 0.218s & 0.238s & 0.248s & $0.92\times$ \\
256  & 6.71M  & 0.765s & 0.742s & 0.765s & $1.03\times$ \\
512  & 26.0M  & 1.046s & 1.157s & 1.277s & $0.90\times$ \\
1024 & 102.3M & 5.040s & \textbf{2.737s} & 3.061s & \boldmath$1.84\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The results support this explanation (Table~\ref{tab:width}). LoRA is 9\% to 12\% slower per step at widths 64 and 128, roughly equal at 256, and about 10\% slower again at 512. At width 1024 (102M parameters), LoRA becomes $1.84\times$ faster at rank 8 and $1.65\times$ faster at rank 64, as the saved gradient computation finally outweighs the adapter cost. Timings at this width vary between repeats (3.7 to 6.1 seconds for full fine-tuning), consistent with a laptop GPU heating up, but the ordering of methods never changes. For RQ4, faster training is not a built-in property of LoRA but depends on model size, and the models in Experiment 3 are well below the crossover.

\section{Experiment 5: KV-Cache Inference}
\label{sec:exp5}

We time generation for the 1.78M-parameter model shape with three methods: no cache (recomputing the full sequence each step), a cache that grows by concatenation, and a preallocated cache written in place. Generation is greedy from a random 16-token prompt with random weights, for 20 to 490 new tokens at batch size 1 or 16, with 10 shuffled repeats per condition.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{KV-cache results at batch size 16 (median of 10 repeats). Speedup is uncached time divided by cached time.}
\label{tab:kvcache}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrr}
\toprule
Tokens & No cache & Concat & Prealloc & Prealloc speedup \\
\midrule
20  & 0.140s & 0.139s ($1.01\times$) & 0.162s & $0.86\times$ \\
50  & 0.342s & 0.257s ($1.33\times$) & 0.320s & $1.07\times$ \\
100 & 0.588s & 0.622s ($0.94\times$) & 0.722s & $0.81\times$ \\
250 & 1.911s & 1.795s ($1.06\times$) & 1.437s & $1.33\times$ \\
490 & 6.348s & 3.428s ($1.85\times$) & \textbf{3.390s} & \boldmath$1.87\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{results/figs/fig_e5_kvcache_speedup.png}
\caption{KV-cache speedup against generation length at batch size 1 (dotted) and 16 (solid). Values below the dashed line mean caching was slower.}
\label{fig:kvcache}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:kvcache} and Fig.~\ref{fig:kvcache} show two distinct regimes. Up to 100 tokens, caching gives no reliable benefit at either batch size, with speedups between $0.81\times$ and $1.33\times$ and interquartile ranges crossing break-even. At batch size 1, results stay noisy even at 490 tokens (median $1.20\times$). A clear benefit appears only at batch size 16 with 490 tokens, where both caches are about $1.86\times$ faster. Memory follows the same pattern: at batch size 16, uncached peak memory grows from 30MB to 108MB with generation length, because every step reprocesses the full sequence, while the preallocated cache holds it constant at 93.4MB. The cached keys and values themselves occupy only 66MB even in the largest configuration. For RQ5, KV caching is neither useless nor automatically beneficial at this scale; it pays off only for long, batched generation.

\section{What the Models Write}
\label{sec:samples}

To see what the failures look like, we reloaded the seed-0 models from Experiment 3 (each reproduces its stored perplexity to three decimal places) and sampled from each method on held-out prompts (temperature 0.8, top-$k$ 50). Table~\ref{tab:samples} shows one representative example.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Unfiltered samples for the prompt ``\texttt{Words: borrow, crystal, strong Summary: Lily accidentally breaks her friend's crystal necklace\ldots\ Story:}''.}
\label{tab:samples}
\begin{tabular}{p{2.4cm}p{13.4cm}}
\toprule
Method & Generated continuation (trimmed) \\
\midrule
No fine-tuning & ``That's important to be careful when borrows, okay?'' A fragment; the base model does not recognize the format. \emph{Words used: borrow.} \\
LoRA $r{=}64$ & ``Once upon a time, there was a little girl named Lily\ldots\ She tried the borrow but she couldn't catch it\ldots\ it was too strong\ldots\ and she went to the park to get her crystal.'' All three words appear, but used almost meaninglessly. \emph{Words used: all three.} \\
Full fine-tuning & ``Once upon a time, there was a little girl named Lily\ldots\ she went to the park to play. She saw a big train\ldots'' Fluent and on-genre, but ignores the required words. \emph{Words used: none.} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Three observations from the samples agree with the metrics. First, adaptation clearly works: every fine-tuned model opens with ``Once upon a time'' and reuses names and objects from the prompt's summary, which the base model never does. Second, constraint following is as weak as the Words rate suggests. When required words appear at all, they are often forced into the sentence ungrammatically, as in ``the borrow.'' Third, the most common failures are repetitive loops, stories that continue past their natural ending into a new \texttt{Summary:} header, and occasional garbled \texttt{\^{a}\texteuro} sequences inherited from the source data (Section~\ref{sec:setup}).

\section{Discussion}

The five experiments share a common theme: efficiency is not a single quantity. Parameter count, unique data, training time, inference time, memory, and quality changed independently, and most expectations carried over from large-scale work needed a qualification at this scale. Capacity scaling was the one expectation that transferred cleanly, following a smooth power law down to 75K parameters. Data repetition, often assumed to be costly, was nearly free up to twelve epochs. LoRA delivered its parameter savings and a small reduction in forgetting, but it trained faster only once the model was wide enough, at which point the same code ran $1.84\times$ faster. KV caching gave no benefit for short generations but a clear $1.87\times$ speedup for long, batched ones, where it also capped memory, which matters more than speed on a 6GB GPU.

We consider the two crossovers the most useful results. In both cases a common rule of thumb (that fewer trainable parameters make training faster, and that caching makes generation faster) quietly depends on scale, and the required conditions are usually met at the scales where these techniques are discussed. We do not claim that LoRA is slower in general or that caching never helps small models. We claim only that, in this controlled setup, these benefits appeared at measurable points rather than by default.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

(1) Test metrics cover a fixed initial portion of each test split (the first 2M of 9.17M TinyStories test tokens in Experiments 1 and 2, and the first 1M tokens of each test set in Experiment 3), which is 22\% to 40\% of each split. (2) Experiment 1 logged metrics on the test split instead of the development split during training. No decision used these values, since the learning rate was fixed and there was no early stopping, so the results stand. (3) Experiment 1's pools are prefixes of the training file rather than random samples, and its learning rate was not tuned per pool size, so the flat region from 8M to 100M tokens could hide an interaction with the learning rate. (4) All results come from one laptop GPU with visible thermal drift, and Experiments 4 and 5 use random weights and vary only width at a fixed depth of 8 layers. (5) The Words rate uses 500 prompts with one sample each, so differences near 1\% are noise and only the floor and ceiling are firm. (6) The power law is fitted to seven points with wide intervals and should not be extrapolated. (7) The 1,024-token vocabulary, single domain, 512-token context, and uncorrected encoding errors limit how far the conclusions generalize. (8) Training-time memory for LoRA compared with full fine-tuning was not measured.

\section{Conclusion}

With a fixed budget of 100M training tokens, adding unique data beyond about 8M tokens brought no further benefit: twelve epochs of repetition had no measurable cost, and fifty epochs cost about 2\% in perplexity. Test loss decreased smoothly from 75K to 8.8M parameters along a power law, with no sign of a floor. LoRA recovered up to 98\% of the perplexity gain of full fine-tuning (at rank 64) while training a fraction of the parameters, and it forgot less. At this width, however, it trained more slowly than full fine-tuning, and its speed advantage appeared only between embedding widths 512 and 1024, in models larger than 26M parameters. No form of adaptation produced real constraint following; the models learned the genre but not the instructions. KV caching was worthwhile only for long, batched generations, where it also limited peak memory.

Each of these findings is narrow and measured, but together they support a broader point. Efficiency techniques come with conditions that are often invisible at the scale where they were developed, and checking them again at the scale where they will actually be used is inexpensive enough to be worth doing. Natural next steps include evaluating on full test splits, adding more model families and seeds, benchmarking on other hardware, using realistic prompt distributions for inference, and cleaning the encoding errors in TinyStories.

